UID:6a429fd1ed35742f00ff275d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This\n program helps people stay active\, make friends\, learn
  new skills\, and \nhave fun. It involves peer-led group activities which 
 can include crafts\, games\,\n walking\, yoga\, and more. Meet new folks\
 , build social connections\, and tell us\n about programs and services you
  would like to see in the neighbourhood!\nOn Tuesdays\, join us for crafts
  (11am-1pm) a walk (11:15am-1pm) and chair yoga (11:30am-12:30pm).\nSnacks
  and refreshments are provided for all participants. Ingredient lists will
  be available at the program.\nThis program is in collaboration with\nGer
 stein Crisis Centre's F.R.E.S.H. initiative\n.
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Varies by program
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a429fd1ed35742f00ff275d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Business Inc. Women Entrepreneurs Series - Information Session
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261017T103000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261017T120000
UID:6a62458ac7e02e3d006b233c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join our Fall 2026 Business Inc. Women Entrepreneurs Informati
 on Session to learn about this 4-week program supporting Toronto entrepren
 eurs to launch and grow.\nDo you have a small business idea and want tailo
 red feedback and support to take it to the next level? Join the Business I
 nc. program to connect with experienced mentors and other entrepreneurs th
 at will help you grow.\nDuring four weekly sessions\, women entrepreneurs 
 will learn business fundamentals\, get clarity on the viability of their b
 usiness ideas\, get feedback and reviews from professional business adviso
 rs\, discuss with and learn from their peers\, and build a foundation for 
 success.\nThe program runs weekly at the S. Walter Stewart Branch on Satur
 days from November 14 - December 5\, from 10am-4pm.\nJoin us at our Inform
 ation Session to learn more about this program\, meet the instructor\, and
  ask any questions you may have about your application.\nLearn about\nBusi
 ness Inc.\n\, a four-week business program offered in partnership with the
  Toronto Business Development Centre.\nWe encourage new or aspiring women 
 entrepreneurs to register\, including those who are cis\, trans and gender
  diverse. Space is limited and registration is required. To register\, cl
 ick\nhere\n.\nThis program is generously supported by Economic Development
  & Culture\, Meridian\, and Toronto Public Library Foundation.
LOCATION:S. Walter Stewart\, Meeting Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a62458ac7e02e3d006b233c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Connections by Andrea Eslava
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260702T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260730T235959
UID:6a42b67e523d340f7c5b6f49@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of July\, the works of artist Andrea Esla
 va are on display at the Yorkville Branch in an exhibit titled Connection
 s.\nAndrea Eslava\, a visual artist based in Toronto\, explores abstractio
 n through colour\, form\, and emotion. Influenced by ancestral teachings\,
  her work invites reflection on the space between the natural landscape an
 d presence. As the mother of a child with a brain injury\, she translates 
 resilience and reconnection into paintings that reflect nature and transf
 ormation.\n"My work explores the connections between the outer and the inn
 er: nature and vibration\, where abstraction invites presence — a breath
 \, a memory beyond words. At its core lies a dialogue between light and e
 motion\, connection and thought: synapses\, echoing my daily routine of nu
 rturing new neural pathways in my daughter’s brain. Through colour as s
 ymbol\, I embody stillness\, renewal\, resilience\, and a sense of belongi
 ng to something greater than ourselves."\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\n
 Art Exhibits Space\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space for loc
 al artists to exhibit their work at locations across the city. These exhib
 its aim to reflect the diverse cultural interests of the city and its neig
 hbourhoods.\nThe exhibit can be viewed during Yorkville branch open hours
 . Please check the\nYorkville\nbranch page before your visit.\nFree. All
  are welcome.\nImage credit: Andrea Eslava\; "Untitled"\; acrylic on canva
 s
LOCATION:Yorkville\, Art exhibit space on main floor.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a42b67e523d340f7c5b6f49
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dufferin/St. Clair Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260804T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260804T200000
UID:6958356a1e64afd01e52048f@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us the first Tuesday of each month to discuss books in a 
 fun and friendly atmosphere.\nCopies are available for pickup at the Duffe
 rin/St. Clair branch each month\, or place your own hold to have a copy se
 nt to your closest branch.\nAugust:\nThe Covenant of Water\nby Abraham Ve
 rghese\nSeptember:\nPurity\nby Jonathan Frazen\nOctober:\nThe Edible Woman
 \nby Margaret Atwood\nNovember:\nSlammerkin\nby Emma Donoghue\nDecember:\
 nWhat We Can Know\nby Ian McEwan
LOCATION:Dufferin/St.Clair\, Earlscourt Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6958356a1e64afd01e52048f
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Writers Collective of Canada (WCC):  Community Writing Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260825T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260825T200000
UID:6a5974e0f213992f00c4e0d2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Experience the transformative power of writing in an inclusive
  space!\nWriters Collective of Canada (WCC) workshops are unlike any other
 . We write together\, share first drafts in an encouraging environment\, l
 isten deeply to one another\, and receive supportive feedback. Tap into yo
 ur story and explore your voice in an environment of dignity\, courage\, a
 nd respect. (wcc-cec.org)\nNo experience required. Beginners are welcome. 
 Materials are provided.\nWorkshops are not sequential\; every workshop is 
 unique - join any session and let's write together!\nSpace is limited\n. R
 egister online\, in-branch or by calling 416-393-7680.\nInformation Collec
 tion Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the To
 ronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(
 c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program re
 gistration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library hand
 les your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tp
 l.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Palmerston\, Program Room (downstairs)
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5974e0f213992f00c4e0d2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
