BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Colson Whitehead: Cool Machine
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260805T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260805T200000
UID:6a26dfeb8d1b82ca9f7869e8@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Two-time Pulitzer Prize-winner Colson Whitehead discusses the 
 magnificent final volume of his Harlem Trilogy.\nCool Machine returns us t
 o the world of successful business owner/master fence Ray Carney as he gam
 bles on one last heist to support his beloved wife\, but finds himself ent
 angled with a legendary criminal mastermind.\nStill haunted by his inabili
 ty to save his cousin Freddie years earlier\, Carney comes out of retireme
 nt to team up with friend and partner in crime\, Pepper\, for a chance to 
 rescue Freddie’s son from the violent forces of the city\, even if it me
 ans risking the safety and security he’s spent decades building for his 
 family.\nWith his trademark pitch-perfect prose\, Whitehead magnificently 
 brings to life 1980s New York\, painting a portrait of a city and a family
  in transition.\nColson Whitehead speaks with Elamin Abdelmahmoud about C
 ool Machine and the journey of bringing his acclaimed\, bestselling trilog
 y to its triumphant conclusion.\nQ&A and book signing to follow. Books ava
 ilable for purchase.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nColson Whitehead\nElam
 in Abdelmahmoud\nRead more:\nCool Machine : A Novel\nCrook Manifesto\nHarl
 em Shuffle\nSon of Elsewhere\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and B
 luma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floo
 r\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registr
 ation required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that t
 he greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guaran
 teed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first come\, first
  served.\nBar:\nBeer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit only).\n***
 \nThis event is part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we host loca
 l and international authors\, artists and thinkers in conversation about t
 heir new books and big ideas.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe pe
 rsonal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library
  under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The 
 information will only be used for event and program registration\, deliver
 y and evaluation. Registrants may be contacted to fill out a brief survey 
 following the program. Questions about how the library handles your inform
 ation can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5
 981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram and Bluma Appel Salon\, 2nd Floo
 r
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a26dfeb8d1b82ca9f7869e8
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Pitch Perfect
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260829T133000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260829T150000
UID:6a5668b5aa34e23600ef0ddc@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:You've spent 8 weeks building your business story\, getting cl
 ear on your customer\, and figuring out your offer. Now it's time to say i
 t out loud. This session teaches you how to craft a clear\, confident 1-mi
 nute pitch for your business - one that actually lands.\nMeet the Resident
 :\nVivian Kaye is a serial entrepreneur and business mentor who bootstrapp
 ed KinkyCurlyYaki to seven figures without outside funding. Over 15 years\
 , she has mentored 300+ entrepreneurs\, recently facilitating workshops fo
 r FoundHers Innovation Labs and Humber College's Entrepreneurship Program.
 \nFeatured in The New York Times\, Toronto Star\, and on CityTV's Cityline
 \, Vivian has partnered with brands including Shopify\, Amazon\, and Ameri
 can Express Canada. Her direct\, practical approach helps early-stage Blac
 k entrepreneurs navigate the realities of building sustainable businesses 
 without traditional support systems.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe 
 personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Libra
 ry under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). Th
 e information will only be used for event and program registration\, deliv
 ery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your informati
 on can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5
 981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Mount Dennis\, Meeting Room 2
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5668b5aa34e23600ef0ddc
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Funding\, Grants & Growth Opportunities For Women Entrepreneurs
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260811T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260811T200000
UID:6a2d5e757550c8bf9f5d1521@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Finding the right funding can be one of the biggest challenges
  facing entrepreneurs. Fortunately\, there are a growing number of grants\
 , programs\, and support organizations designed to help women entrepreneur
 s start and grow their businesses.\nThis workshop provides an overview of 
 funding pathways\, grant opportunities\, community resources\, and busines
 s support programs. Participants will leave with a better understanding of
  what resources are available and where to begin their search for funding 
 and growth opportunities.\nAll sessions are interactive and participants a
 re encouraged to bring questions related to their own business or entrepre
 neurial goals.\nMeet the Resident\n: Melanie Cruickshank is a founder and 
 brand-side operator with over 15 years of experience building\, launching\
 , and scaling beauty and personal care brands. As the founder of da lish c
 osmetics\, she led everything from product development and retail strategy
  to e-commerce growth and national partnerships. Known for her hands-on\, 
 no-nonsense approach\, Melanie brings a founder’s accountability and a d
 eep understanding of what it takes to build brands that perform in market.
 \nThis program is part of Toronto Public Library’s\nEntrepreneur in Resi
 dence\nprogram series with a focus on women entrepreneurs\, designed to su
 pport women as they explore entrepreneurship and build practical small bus
 iness skills. Participants will learn about key topics for starting and gr
 owing a business\, with guidance from an experienced entrepreneur in resid
 ence. For more information and a full list of programs\, visit\ntpl.ca/eir
 \n.
LOCATION:S. Walter Stewart\, Meeting Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a2d5e757550c8bf9f5d1521
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
