  (11am-1pm) a walk (11:15am-1pm) and chair yoga (11:30am-12:30pm).\nSnacks
  and refreshments are provided for all participants. Ingredient lists will
  be available at the program.\nThis program is in collaboration with\nGer
 stein Crisis Centre's F.R.E.S.H. initiative\n.
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Varies by program
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a429fd1ed35742f00ff2754
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Michelle Parsons
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260702T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260731T235959
UID:6a551f4964ed854800a5471e@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of July\, works by artist Michelle Parson
 s are on display at the Forest Hill branch.\nMichelle Parsons has lived in
  Toronto for over two decades and is originally from Newfoundland and Labr
 ador. She holds a Chemical Engineering degree from the University of Toron
 to and previously had a 15-year career in manufacturing. Michelle's fascin
 ation with bird life was sparked when she received binoculars as a gift\, 
 leading to many bird-spotting adventures in the city’s parks. Now dedica
 ted to bridging the gap between art and science\, Michelle is training to 
 become a science illustrator.\n"As a science illustrator\, I am fascinat
 ed by the avian biodiversity in Toronto. By using a variety of references 
 and field observations\, my goals are to capture the colourful personality
  of each bird and to highlight that nature is all around us. I'd like my 
 work to serve as an educational tool and a call to action for environmenta
 l stewardship. I use watercolour\, cold-pressed paper and occasionally cy
 anotype to capture the unique traits of our feathered friends and their ha
 bitats."\nThis exhibit is part of\nTPL's Art Exhibit\nprogram. Toronto Pub
 lic Library provides space for local artists to exhibit their work at loca
 tions across the city. These exhibits aim to reflect the diverse cultural 
 interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nThis exhibit can be viewed 
 during the Forest Hill branch's open hours. Please check the\nForest Hill\
 nbranch page before you visit.\nFree. All are welcome.\nImage credit: Mic
 helle Parsons\; "King Eider Duck"\; watercolour
LOCATION:Forest Hill
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a551f4964ed854800a5471e
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Don Mills Poetry Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260901T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20260901T200000
UID:6a5e3f73a41369ab2c674cea@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Do you write poetry? Want to meet other poets in the community
  in a friendly\, supportive environment to air your work and share feedbac
 k\, encouragement and inspiration? Join a group of local poets from Septem
 ber to December in our Don Mills Poetry Circle program.\nPlease come to ea
 ch session with a printed copy of a poem based on the following prompts:\n
 Tuesday\, September 1 - Autumn\nTuesday\, October 6 - Bliss: What's Your
 s?\nTuesday\, November 3 - Blood\nTuesday\, December 1 - The Holiday Sea
 son\nRegistration is limited at 12 participants.
LOCATION:Don Mills\, Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a5e3f73a41369ab2c674cea
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
